--TEST--
Spec § 4: `Core\Time\Date::at` bounds each of its three fields on both sides — year -9999/9999 against -10000/10000, month 1/12 against 0/13, day 1/31 against 0/32 — the day's own ceiling is read from the other two fields rather than fixed, and past every calendar bound there is a second one, a field wider than the calendar writes, with its own sentence and its own pair of neighbours
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 4: `at` is the only way to write a `Date`'s three fields, so the bounds it
// enforces are the whole of what a calendar date can be. Each is asserted on
// both sides: a member that stopped one entry early prints plausibly against
// either half alone, and a member that clamped instead of refusing prints
// plausibly against the accepted half.
//
// Unlike the clock's four fields, a date's three do not have independent
// ceilings — February's last day is a fact about the year — so the sweep below
// is over (year, month) pairs and not over a fixed row.
class Cal {
    // The rendering, or the refusal, as one string. Both halves of every bound
    // below go through this, so an accepted value and a refused one are told
    // apart by what came back and never by which line printed.
    public static function at(int $year, int $month, int $day): string {
        try {
            return Core\Time\Date::at($year, $month as uint, $day as uint)->format("yyyy-MM-dd");
        } catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
            return $refused->message;
        }
    }

    public static function accepted(int $year, int $month, int $day): bool {
        return !Core\Str::contains(Cal::at($year, $month, $day), "Core\\Time\\Date::at()");
    }

    // The second bound, the one past the calendar: a number no calendar field
    // can hold at all. `date_from_parts` narrows the three `int`s to the
    // widths `civil::Date::new` takes *before* the calendar sees them, so a
    // field past that width is refused by a different sentence — one naming
    // the three fields together rather than the one that spoiled the date.
    public static function narrowed(int $year, int $month, int $day): bool {
        return Core\Str::contains(
            Cal::at($year, $month, $day),
            "a month and a day are numbers a calendar writes",
        );
    }
}

// The accepted side, one field at a time: each row puts a single field at an
// end and the others at their floor, so a bound read off the wrong field shows
// up as a rendering rather than as a missing refusal. The year has two ends
// and the other two fields have one apiece, because a month and a day are
// counted from 1 and a year is not counted at all.
echo "year-lo=", Cal::at(-9999, 1, 1), "|\n";
echo "year-hi=", Cal::at(9999, 1, 1), "|\n";
echo "month-hi=", Cal::at(2024, 12, 1), "|\n";
echo "day-hi=", Cal::at(2024, 1, 31), "|\n";
// All three at an end at once, which is where the bounds are simultaneously
// reachable — so they are not three separate claims about three separate
// dates.
echo "all-three=", Cal::at(9999, 12, 31), "|\n";

// The refused side, one past each end, in the same shape. The floors are
// reached here and the clock's are not: `at`'s two `uint` fields still refuse
// zero, because a calendar counts its months and days from one. A negative
// month never reaches the member at all — that is `E0401: expected `uint`,
// found `int`` in the checker, and a case that must fail to compile is a
// different file.
echo "year-past-lo=", Cal::at(-10000, 1, 1), "|\n";
echo "year-past-hi=", Cal::at(10000, 1, 1), "|\n";
echo "month-zero=", Cal::at(2024, 0, 1), "|\n";
echo "month-past-hi=", Cal::at(2024, 13, 1), "|\n";
echo "day-zero=", Cal::at(2024, 1, 0), "|\n";
echo "day-past-hi=", Cal::at(2024, 1, 32), "|\n";

// Which field the sentence names, when more than one is past its end. Unlike
// the clock's constructor — whose four fields arrive in one options bag and
// are refused in one sentence naming the whole reading — a date's three are
// checked in the calendar's own precedence, and the sentence names the
// outermost field that is wrong: the year before the month, the month before
// the day. That is the same precedence `compareTo` orders by.
echo "all-past=", Cal::at(10000, 13, 32), "|\n";
echo "two-past=", Cal::at(2024, 13, 32), "|\n";

// The day's ceiling is not a number: it is read from the year and the month
// together, which is the whole of why § 4 has no `checkdate`. Six (year,
// month) pairs, each with the last day it accepts — including both halves of
// the century rule, where 2000 is a leap year and 1900 is not — and each
// asserted on both sides: the last day accepted, the next one refused. A
// member holding one fixed ceiling per month passes the first four rows and
// fails 1900; a member holding 31 everywhere fails all twelve counts.
array<int> $years = [2024, 2023, 2000, 1900, 2024, 2024];
array<int> $months = [2, 2, 2, 2, 4, 1];
array<int> $lasts = [29, 28, 29, 28, 30, 31];
int $row = 0;
int $lastAccepted = 0;
int $nextRefused = 0;
string $ceilings = "";
for (; $row < 6; $row = $row + 1) {
    int $year = $years[$row as string];
    int $month = $months[$row as string];
    int $last = $lasts[$row as string];
    if (Cal::accepted($year, $month, $last)) {
        $lastAccepted = $lastAccepted + 1;
    }
    if (!Cal::accepted($year, $month, $last + 1)) {
        $nextRefused = $nextRefused + 1;
    }
    $ceilings = $ceilings . Cal::at($year, $month, $last) . " ";
}
echo "last-accepted=", $lastAccepted as string,
    " next-refused=", $nextRefused as string, "|\n";
echo $ceilings, "|\n";
// And the refusal says which month it is talking about, so a caller reading it
// learns the ceiling rather than only that 29 was wrong.
echo "leap-sentence=", Cal::at(2023, 2, 29), "|\n";

// The second bound, past the calendar's. Every field has one, every one of
// them is one entry wide, and the sentence changes across it: 32,767 is a year
// the calendar refuses by name and 32,768 is a number no year field holds;
// 127 is a month the calendar refuses by name and 128 is not a month at all.
// Both halves are asserted, because a single refusal proves nothing about
// where the two sentences part.
echo "year-widest-calendar=", (!Cal::narrowed(32767, 1, 1)
    && !Cal::accepted(32767, 1, 1)) as string,
    " year-past-width=", Cal::narrowed(32768, 1, 1) as string,
    " year-widest-calendar-lo=", (!Cal::narrowed(-32768, 1, 1)
        && !Cal::accepted(-32768, 1, 1)) as string,
    " year-past-width-lo=", Cal::narrowed(-32769, 1, 1) as string, "|\n";
echo "month-widest-calendar=", (!Cal::narrowed(2024, 127, 1)
    && !Cal::accepted(2024, 127, 1)) as string,
    " month-past-width=", Cal::narrowed(2024, 128, 1) as string,
    " day-widest-calendar=", (!Cal::narrowed(2024, 1, 127)
        && !Cal::accepted(2024, 1, 127)) as string,
    " day-past-width=", Cal::narrowed(2024, 1, 128) as string, "|\n";
// A field far past everything is refused by that same second sentence rather
// than taken modulo anything: month 100,000 is not month 4 of some later year,
// which is the difference between a constructor and the step `plus` takes.
echo "far-past=", Cal::at(2024, 100000, 1), "|\n";
--EXPECT--
year-lo=-9999-01-01|
year-hi=9999-01-01|
month-hi=2024-12-01|
day-hi=2024-01-31|
all-three=9999-12-31|
year-past-lo=Core\Time\Date::at(): parameter 'year' is not in the required range of -9999..=9999|
year-past-hi=Core\Time\Date::at(): parameter 'year' is not in the required range of -9999..=9999|
month-zero=Core\Time\Date::at(): parameter 'month' is not in the required range of 1..=12|
month-past-hi=Core\Time\Date::at(): parameter 'month' is not in the required range of 1..=12|
day-zero=Core\Time\Date::at(): parameter 'day' is not in the required range of 1..=31|
day-past-hi=Core\Time\Date::at(): parameter 'day' for `2024-01` is invalid, must be in range `1..=31`|
all-past=Core\Time\Date::at(): parameter 'year' is not in the required range of -9999..=9999|
two-past=Core\Time\Date::at(): parameter 'month' is not in the required range of 1..=12|
last-accepted=6 next-refused=6|
2024-02-29 2023-02-28 2000-02-29 1900-02-28 2024-04-30 2024-01-31 |
leap-sentence=Core\Time\Date::at(): parameter 'day' for `2023-02` is invalid, must be in range `1..=28`|
year-widest-calendar=1 year-past-width=1 year-widest-calendar-lo=1 year-past-width-lo=1|
month-widest-calendar=1 month-past-width=1 day-widest-calendar=1 day-past-width=1|
far-past=Core\Time\Date::at(): a year is within -9999..=9999, and a month and a day are numbers a calendar writes|
